Part 2 – Kubernetes
1. What is Kubernetes?
Kubernetes (K8s) is a container orchestration platform that automates deployment, scaling, networking, health monitoring and lifecycle management of containerized applications.
OpenShift is built on top of Kubernetes and extends it with enterprise features such as security, routes, image streams, operators and integrated CI/CD.
2. Kubernetes Architecture
Control Plane (Master Nodes)
Responsible for managing the cluster.
Components include:
API Server
Scheduler
Controller Manager
etcd (stores cluster state)
Worker Nodes
Responsible for running applications.
Each worker contains:
kubelet
kube-proxy
Container Runtime (CRI-O in OpenShift)
Pods
3. Kubernetes Objects
Everything in Kubernetes is represented as an object.
Common objects:
Pod
ReplicaSet
Deployment
Service
ConfigMap
Secret
PersistentVolume
PersistentVolumeClaim
StatefulSet
DaemonSet
Job
CronJob
Namespace
These objects are usually created declaratively using YAML.
4. Pods
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
A pod may contain:
One application container
Multiple helper (sidecar) containers
All containers inside a pod share:
Network namespace
IP address
localhost communication
Volumes
Pods are ephemeral.
If deleted, Kubernetes creates a new pod.
Never depend on pod lifetime.
5. Pod Lifecycle
Typical flow:
Pending
    ↓
ContainerCreating
    ↓
Running
    ↓
Succeeded / Failed
Pods are recreated rather than repaired.
6. ReplicaSet
Purpose:
Maintain desired number of pod replicas.
Example:
Desired replicas = 3
If one pod crashes
ReplicaSet automatically creates another.
ReplicaSet only maintains count.
Deployment manages ReplicaSets.
7. Deployment
Deployment manages ReplicaSets.
Relationship:
Deployment
      │
      ├── ReplicaSet-1
      │       ├── Pod
      │       ├── Pod
      │       └── Pod
      │
      └── ReplicaSet-2
              ├── Pod
              └── Pod
Trainer note:
Deployment → One-to-Many → ReplicaSets
ReplicaSet → One-to-Many → Pods
Think of it like a tree.
8. Declarative vs Imperative
Trainer strongly recommended:
Always use Declarative style.
Imperative
kubectl run
kubectl expose
Declarative
deployment.yaml
service.yaml

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
Advantages
Version controlled
Repeatable
Git friendly
CI/CD friendly
9. YAML Best Practices
YAML is indentation sensitive.
Even one extra space may fail parsing.
Generate YAML whenever possible instead of writing from scratch.
10. Labels & Selectors
Labels are key-value pairs attached to resources.
Example
app=payment

env=prod

team=cps
Selectors use labels to identify resources.
ReplicaSets and Services locate Pods using selectors.
11. Namespaces
Namespaces logically isolate resources.
Example:
default

payments

openshift-etcd

kube-system
Resources with same name can exist in different namespaces.
12. Services
Pods are temporary.
Services provide stable communication.
Types covered in training:
ClusterIP
Default.
Accessible only inside cluster.
Used for:
Microservice communication
App → Database
NodePort
Exposes service on every node.
<Node-IP>:<NodePort>
Even if Pod moves,
NodePort remains accessible.
Trainer command
oc describe svc <service-name>
shows allocated NodePort.
LoadBalancer
Mostly used in managed OpenShift (AWS/Azure).
Cloud provider allocates external IP.
In bare-metal environments,
MetalLB Operator is required.
13. Kubernetes Scheduling
Developer does NOT decide which node runs a pod.
Scheduler decides automatically.
When scaling,
Pods may land on different nodes.
14. Why Applications Should Not Store Data Inside Pods
Pods are temporary.
If a pod is deleted,
its filesystem is lost.
Persistent data should always be stored externally.
Examples:
Database
Persistent Volume
Object Storage
15. Stateless vs Stateful
Stateless
Examples:
REST APIs
Payment APIs
Frontend
Any replica can serve requests.
Pods can be replaced anytime.
Stateful
Examples:
Databases
Kafka
MongoDB
MySQL
Need persistent identity and storage.
Use StatefulSets.
16. StatefulSet (Trainer Discussion)
Used when:
Stable Pod names required
Persistent storage required
Ordered startup/shutdown required
Example:
mysql-0

mysql-1

mysql-2
Trainer demonstrated WordPress + MySQL.
Only one pod can acquire ReadWriteOnce PVC at a time.
Scaling requires StatefulSets.
17. Kubernetes Best Practices
Never depend on Pod IP.
Never store application state inside Pods.
Use Deployments for stateless apps.
Use StatefulSets for stateful apps.
Everything should be managed through YAML.
Prefer declarative deployments.
Label resources properly.
Services communicate using selectors.
Pods are disposable.
Trainer Nuggets:
"Think of Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods as a tree."
"Always follow declarative style."
"YAML is strict with indentation."
"Developer should not care where the pod runs; Kubernetes scheduler decides."
"Pods are cattle, not pets."
